## Existing content and review

Preserve existing differing, stale, arbitrary, unsafe, symlinked, and nonregular runbooks rather than overwriting them by inference. Review exact ownership, the nine-field record, permissions, stop conditions, rollback, and evidence before accepting a new static document. A `permissions` field describes the project-approved boundary; it does not grant capability.

## Runbook sections

A qualifying runbook should make the following visible in ordinary Markdown:

- purpose and trigger;
- exact reads and prerequisites;
- ordered actions owned by the project;
- stop conditions and refusal cases;
- required permissions and external boundaries;
- observable evidence and expected result; and
- rollback or recovery conditions.

Keep project-specific commands in the runbook only when the project has confirmed them. Keep credentials and secret values out of the file. Link back to `OPERATIONS.md`, the governing Plan, and relevant decisions or quality evidence.

## Relationship to capability artifacts

A static runbook may support `docs/FRONTEND.md` client-side behavior or `docs/RELIABILITY.md` service/runtime operations only when the applicable capability is explicitly Confirmed; it never changes those documents' scope. `docs/RELIABILITY.md`, when present, supplements and remains subordinate to `OPERATIONS.md`, while `DESIGN.md` remains universal and visual/interaction/accessibility focused. Keep procedure evidence, capability evidence, audit observations, setup actions, and Plan state distinct; cite the owner, source, exact project-owned verification, and residual risk.
